00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:21.349 align:start position:0%
 
thanks um  okay so i just want to start by  introducing myself uh i'm cindy i am uh  currently an assistant professor in  statistics at columbia before that i was  at yale where i got my phd and this is  what i look like without a mask on um  i this is my second semester at simon's  i was also here virtually  last fall for the program on probability  geometry and computation in high  dimensions  um so i just wanted to take the like 10  minutes today to give you sort of an  overview um about my research obviously  it's a short talk so i can't go into any  details but i'm here until december and  i sit upstairs and i'm always happy to  talk if you want to talk more so  kind of the short story is that my  research uses tools and ideas from a lot  of different fields like  information theory statistical physics  signal processing and applied  probability  in order to understand um  kind of modern high dimensional  statistical and inference statistical  inference and estimation problems  and also like a complex machine learning  task  um  so the thrust of my research isn't  answering questions like uh the  following so if we're given some like i  don't know hard stats problem  uh how much information or how much data  is necessary and sufficient to solve  that problem and then you know given  that data how do we most effectively use  it to gain insight on the problem at  hand and can we quantify the limitations  of that insight  or finally you know can we develop and  analyze  um  uh computationally efficient algorithms  to perform the sort of inference and  estimation  and so um with those questions in mind  uh i'm going to talk about kind of two  like overarching focuses uh of my my  research uh the first is on  understanding  uh fundamental limits of statistical uh  recovery  um and then you know if possible trying  to find computationally efficient  methods to perform at those limits so  this is kind of like  uh just core what like the program  uh i'm joining is about uh and then the  the second uh  part of my research is um  that i'm going to talk about today is on  establishing uh  sort of rigorous theoretical uh  performance guarantees for various  methods and procedures and high  dimensional uh statistics  okay so let's talk a little bit about  fundamental limits first so  uh information theorists have long  studied uh sort of establishing  fundamental limits on uh the transfer of  data in order to design you know  practical communication systems right  this is like core information theory  uh these days right it's it's obvious  that these sorts of ideas and  methodologies uh important in like sort  of any data-driven field where uh one  wishes to you know uh gain as much  insight from uh the data as possible so  for example you know statistics and  machine learning and so in these fields  you know  these data-driven fields it's now  important to establish computational and  information theoretic limits of problems  so along this line of work  i kind of i focus on two areas so uh one  is just kind of the like classic  information theory application  of like wireless communication so uh  in short what is the communications like  challenge right uh the idea is we want  to send some information over a noisy  channel and we want to send information  over the channel in a way that it's  reliable so you know uh the person at  the end of the channel understands what  you're saying uh we want to send the  information with data rates approaching  the channel capacity which is this  information theoretic limit and we want  to do this all in a computationally  efficient way so that you know our cell  phones like work in real time  right and so um  the kind of the the long story short of  how folks do this is uh  they um  build encoding and decoding schemes at  either end of the channel and so the  encoder will transmit a signal uh where  they've added some redundancy in order  to combat the noise in the channel and  then the decoder will take the output  and denoise it in some way  so um  my work specifically has looked at uh  sparse regression codes which is an  encoding scheme for various channel  models it kind of leverages the work  from the sparse signal recovery  community  in order to sort of challenge this uh in  order to tackle this uh communications  challenge  so the idea is that uh we encode our  information in a sparse vector  and then um you know uh when you send  that over uh the measurements of that  sparse vector over a channel the  decoding task um then becomes a high  dimensional linear regression problem  but one where the the number of you know  columns in your measurement matrix is  growing more quickly than the number of  rows  so um  in particular my my work has um  developed an approximate message passing  uh decoding scheme for sparse regression  codes  and um  the the basic idea is that you know if  we can add enough sort of structure to  the signal itself and uh this is  something we can do because it's a  designed problem right so i have control  over the signal and the measurements and  these things but if i can add enough  structure to the signal um relating to  the sparsity then i can sort of  rigorously show that amp will uh recover  the the true signal  um  in as few measurements as information  theoretically possible so uh in this you  know in the the language of  communications we are coding at the  channel capacity and so um i studied  this originally for just the standard  added of white now gaussian noise  channel which is like your  vanilla uh channel that you know  everybody learns in like chapter two of  the the information theory textbook um  but it's also used in more  uh complex channels that have kind of  modern applications like internet of  things and  and these sorts of um models i'll also  mention um related to this i have some  work  dealing with phase transitions with  changing sparsity and this is something  i'll talk  a little bit more about in the workshop  next week that sam mentioned earlier  um i just wanted to plug it real quick  now so uh what do i mean by changing  sparsity right um well i don't know for  i don't have to say this to uh uh people  in this room but i will right so um  often in high dimensional problems right  we we assume that the uh  underlying effective dimensionality of  you know the the the problem is much  smaller than the the natural  uh representation um  of of the dimensionality and and so uh  for this reason establishing sort of  information theoretic and computational  limits um in the context of sparsity is  something that researchers have spent a  lot of time doing but  usually um the the sparse models that um  we've studied are those where you know  if we have a sparse vector for example  um  the number of non-zeros  is a a small but like fixed proportion  of the length of the vector  and so um my work here studies uh a true  sparse limit where in fact if we think  about you know the the problem size  growing the sparsity is actually um  approaching uh zero  and so um this is kind of a fun uh  problem uh for me um so in particular  you know what what did i do well um i  was studying computational phase  transitions uh  for uh  rank one um matrix estimation and this  true  uh sparse uh setting and in particular  we were studying uh computational phase  transitions using approximate message  passing and um  i i kind of like this uh problem because  you know  um when we have this vanishing sparsity  we can't use the usual sort of  asymptotic analysis of the approximate  message passing algorithms we instead um  have to use like a finite sample  analysis which is something that i  studied um a lot during my uh phd  okay so just um to wrap up i wanted to  say a little bit about um my work that's  maybe uh less related to  um  this program but i still think is very  cool and um that's on the statistical  analysis of  high dimensional estimators  and so um  okay maybe i'll stay here for a second  um  okay so um what am i what am i i mean  here right well uh clearly like a a  major goal of modern statistics is to  understand um high dimensionality better  right so  uh you know we often find ourselves in  situations where we like to estimate an  unknown signal or maybe an unknown  function and we imagine a model where  you know the number of parameters in the  problem is growing as quickly or more  quickly than the sample size  so um in in those settings right um  a usual way to to estimate that unknown  signal or or  density is um  to minimize some sort of empirical  distance measure between you know uh the  data and then uh the best estimator and  some sort of constraining set so for  example you might constrain your vector  to be sparse or um your function to be  smooth  and it turns out that uh iterative  algorithms um have proven to be very  successful in sort of solving these  constrained optimization problems and  giving us you know uh these these sorts  of um  these high-dimensional estimators  and so uh with these ideas in mind my  research has um studied uh  the the theoretical analysis of  approximate message passing algorithms  um to study these sorts of constrained  optimization problems  um and also uh the use of approximate  message passing  uh  as a tool to give us sort of exact  asymptotic characterizations of these  statistical estimators  so um just a quick word on amp so  uh why why are we talking about amp here  well one of the reasons that um amp is  so powerful in these situations  is that um  through a recursion referred to as state  evolution  uh  the the sort of amp analysis allows for  exact characterization of the  algorithm's asymptotic performance at  every iteration and it turns out that  these sorts of guarantees through the  state evolution can then be used to  establish  exact like high dimensional  characterizations of various statistical  estimators and this is done by  essentially constructing a suitable amp  iteration for your problem one that  solves the the optimization you're  interested in um  and then you can kind of like push the  amp state evolution guarantees uh from  the algorithm on to uh the estimator  itself  and so um  with this in mind my work has studied  you know mathematical analysis of um the  amp algorithms  usually motivated by uh the use of amp  and specific problems where the sort of  standard analysis um  no longer uh  was applicable um and then i've also  studied the the using amp  as a tool to give us um some analysis of  the high dimensional performance of  various estimators in particular um  i've studied l1 penalization a lot um  through uh the lasso and also a  generalization of the lasso called um  the sorted l1 penalty or slope  um okay so that's all uh i was gonna say  today uh i sit upstairs please um come  talk to me if you have any questions  thanks cindy so we have a break before  so save everybody back here of course  yeah

